#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e82e8b16-51a8-59c8-9ed1-51619335de97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d71454-0458-5173-92e2-d24ae7d33b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420761} {:answer/id #uuid "299c3fa6-9fa8-5565-8634-09054fedc6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420762} {:answer/id #uuid "48db3d3c-30c7-55ab-a903-b5f38c30500f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420768} {:answer/id #uuid "96fb9c14-df10-5b90-9b22-46eaa98a547f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420777} {:answer/id #uuid "f34f3108-25b4-52ad-8686-5c2f57c96aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96fb9c14-df10-5b90-9b22-46eaa98a547f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420777} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce23d7e-7b89-51d8-91ae-2e6f5b73477c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7a5359f-8967-570a-b634-daaee8dbc9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-138535" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e82e8b16-51a8-59c8-9ed1-51619335de97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0755beb3-73a6-5399-8b09-ddef07ddf09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420759} {:answer/id #uuid "32b39ef8-9dd6-57b9-ab9a-17113d1beb75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420764} {:answer/id #uuid "579ce615-12df-5c19-81bd-6fbd6731105e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420770} {:answer/id #uuid "890f27c1-0c91-5b46-9f4e-6f30606040c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420776} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e09d3c-fdfb-5162-bbc7-9d78cc139800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "890f27c1-0c91-5b46-9f4e-6f30606040c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420776} :answer-field/id #uuid "22532903-9de5-5ea6-88dc-56a50c21802f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "237a5725-5a0e-5e90-89ea-d6700e3a977b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138563" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff858037-da99-53e9-acdc-e4963e7d765e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1243f8e9-7cde-50e1-b4da-69c3c09e0244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420760} {:answer/id #uuid "396c503f-4112-5172-aabe-628ff629a730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420766} {:answer/id #uuid "71ce3c99-faa6-5850-9bb1-6d482034baca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420771} {:answer/id #uuid "75a82642-78cf-5946-8735-5f599b8bb673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420773} {:answer/id #uuid "d93a36d9-9b8f-5c2d-b424-076056039fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d93a36d9-9b8f-5c2d-b424-076056039fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420780} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3fa58d8-221b-5878-a7cd-de5fff20b644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83e058d9-31bd-5eef-83c8-b6f7c1427845" :question/math-academy-id "q-138579" :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}5\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff858037-da99-53e9-acdc-e4963e7d765e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c5eab7-61e5-58ba-983c-a7f55e21301d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420765} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1203aa-43a1-5ff4-889a-1439cc8945c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420767} {:answer/id #uuid "73f62c7b-39b6-567c-8e60-5bf5c983d0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420772} {:answer/id #uuid "7e01a274-8738-5cf2-a673-1055c4c67b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420775} {:answer/id #uuid "fb51fb6a-1738-5259-951f-347a4793bbda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c1203aa-43a1-5ff4-889a-1439cc8945c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420767} :answer-field/id #uuid "a01449e0-fee7-54b8-bc93-a094ffce75c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a18b0561-3c55-5481-a95c-144e99d62fb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-138620" :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}],\\,{V}_{r}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff858037-da99-53e9-acdc-e4963e7d765e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e553566-a4fb-554e-8652-c4ff524a08ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\fra